[{:seon.task/name "a-pulls-cost-is-a-function-of-its-input"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-10 by the test session (`seon.cost-test/measure-unit :db/pull-attr 30`: a 2-attribute `d/pull` less a 1-attribute pull on one in-memory db): 2,774 ticks and 8,560 B alone in a process; 2,590 and 7,488 after nine other test namespaces ran; 1,976 and 6,768 in a forked child after one warm call. Ticks and bytes move 13-28% with what ran earlier in the process, so a cost proof on the pull path measures its shard's history. Ruled out by reading: Jolt's per-call-site protocol caches (built only with --opt, which bin/build does not pass) and the node cache (an in-memory index is never written). Candidates, each with the one measurement that decides it: the depth of the dynamic-binding stack (a read of a bound var walks it, once an attribute: measure inside a fresh binding of `datahike.index.interface/*reads*`); Datahike's schema cache, shared by every store of the process (reset `datahike.schema-cache/schema-meta-cache` first); where the call sits on its Chez stack segment (a segment overflow allocates a counted continuation: measure at 0, 50 and 200 nested calls). None explains the same pull allocating 1 KB less after other namespaces ran. Next step: a per-function self-bytes profile of the unit, fresh and after the nine namespaces, diffed. Until fixed, the unit table measures in a fresh process."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
]
